tls: use the system OpenSSL instead of a bundled crypto stack

rustls delegates crypto to a provider, and BOTH providers — ring and
aws-lc-rs — ship the same OpenSSL-derived ARM assembly, which emits
`.arch armv7-a` under a guard that is always true for a 32-bit ARM ELF
build. The directive overrides -march, so no compiler flag and no rootfs
avoids it, and it enables Thumb-2 encodings. Measured with
`qemu-arm -cpu arm1176`: the binary dies on `mov.w`, which ARMv6Z does not
implement, while the same binary runs under `-cpu cortex-a7`. Swapping ring
for aws-lc-rs does not help; aws-lc-sys carries the same 20 asm files, and
AWS_LC_SYS_NO_ASM panics outside debug builds.

native-tls links the system libssl instead, so no crypto assembly is built
into the binary at all — the shared library on the target is whatever the
distribution compiled, which on Raspberry Pi OS armhf is ARMv6. This is how
spotifyd and myMPD both ship working v6 binaries: their NEEDED lists show
libssl.so.3. It is also what Debian prefers to a vendored crypto stack, and
it means a libssl flaw is fixed by `apt upgrade` instead of by rebuilding.

Not fighting an upstream decision: the Tauri build used `.use_native_tls()`,
and qbz-qobuz/src/cmaf.rs documents adding the native-tls feature as the
intended escape hatch. No `.use_native_tls()` call is needed — with
default-features off and only native-tls enabled, it is reqwest's only
backend.

Four declarations, since cargo features are additive and one crate asking
for rustls would pull it back in. PR #663 (fengalin) moved reqwest to
`rustls-tls-webpki-roots-no-provider` and installs an aws-lc-rs provider
from qbz-app, which leaves BOTH providers in the graph: aws-lc-sys through
that provider, and ring through qconnect-transport-ws's tokio-tungstenite,
which the PR did not touch. So the provider declaration and its two call
sites go too — with native-tls there is nothing to install.

Verified on the pre-release branch: `cargo tree -p qbzd -e normal` resolves
no ring, no aws-lc-sys and no rustls, and reaches openssl-sys through
native-tls. Cargo.lock is included because the build runs --locked.

